  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis afternoon through Thursday...\n\nThe latest high res models are alluding to more of a drying trend vs \nprevious model runs, and given the mid-level flow becoming more \nsouthwesterly, there will likely only be some isolated thunderstorms \ndeveloping later this afternoon along the mountain/desert interface, \nand then these will move off towards the northeast and skirt across \nthe deserts by later in the afternoon/evening. With much more \ntroughing upstream influencing the region, temperatures will begin a \nnotable cooling trend from this afternoon into tomorrow. MUCAPE \nthrough a consensus of guidance appears to be roughly around 1000 \nJ/kg over the San Diego mountains by later this afternoon, but the \nmajority of the instability (most unstable CAPE) lies just to the \neast of our CWA. Given the parameters, and flow in the mid level, it \nis going to be hard for storms to get going. Though, through \norographic lifting, along with some shear, there could be a few \nstorms, with the most likely location being over the San Bernardino \nmountains on the eastern slopes, and over the San Jacinto area. \nThere may also be a storm popping off over southern San Diego County \nin the Jacumba area. Otherwise, a transition towards cooler and \ndrier conditions are expected going into tomorrow. Winds will also \nincrease more during the day, with gusts approaching 60 mph within \nGorgonio Pass and also gusts of greater than 50 mph across the high \ndeserts. The marine layer should fill in better for tonight in \ncomparison to recent nights as onshore becomes more prevalent, and \nthis will carry into tomorrow morning with increasing ceilings, \nextending further east into the inland areas. As the trough \ncontinues to deepen and propagate closer to the region, it will also \nallow for a \"baroclinic leaf\" to develop and provide and extensive \nmid to high level cloud deck tomorrow, which will also hinder \ndaytime heating, with values becoming around 5 to 10 degrees below \nthe seasonal average for this time of year. The drier air aloft will \ncut off chances of convection for tomorrow.\n\nFriday through the middle of next week...\n\nPersistent onshore flow will prevail into the early part of the \nupcoming weekend and then the ridge to the east will begin to expand \nback towards the west with increasing heights, and thus a gradual \nwarmup back to around seasonal temperatures for this time of year, \nand even a little above average temperatures will return going into \nthe early half of next week as heights creep back above 595 dm. Due \nto this, there may need to be heat products issued for the deserts \nagain by Monday, as temperatures look to soar north of 110F for \nmuch of the lower deserts and within the Coachella Valley. As this\noccurs, there will be some recycled monsoonal moisture that may \nmake its way back into southern California, and may give our \nmountains and deserts a slight chance of afternoon thunderstorms \ntowards the middle of next week, otherwise remaining dry elsewhere\nthrough then.",
